    {
      "dom_id": "question-251821",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14218985/assets/72fdce3ebf5cbdb9e0b06e88e9fd5a519afdd79faedefd0ec24d003a8bc6e557.png)\n\n**From top to bottom**, the **exact** values of the $y$-intercepts of the parabola shown above are{{field-1}}\nand \n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-4,3)$ and passes through the point $(-3,2).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(-4,3)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y-3)^{2}=4p(x+4).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-3,2),$ we substitute $x=-3$ and $y=2$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-3)^{2} & =4p(-3+4) \\\\ 1 & =4p(1) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-3)^{2} & =x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-3)^{2} & =0+4 \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ y-3 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2+3 \\\\ y & =1,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are $5$ and  $1.$",
